<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Benchmarking Result Details</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix C Benchmarking Result Details ‣ Parallel Tokenizers: Rethinking Vocabulary Designfor Cross-Lingual Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, <a href="#A3.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix C Benchmarking Result Details ‣ Parallel Tokenizers: Rethinking Vocabulary Designfor Cross-Lingual Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, <a href="#A3.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Appendix C Benchmarking Result Details ‣ Parallel Tokenizers: Rethinking Vocabulary Designfor Cross-Lingual Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, and <a href="#A3.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix C Benchmarking Result Details ‣ Parallel Tokenizers: Rethinking Vocabulary Designfor Cross-Lingual Transfer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> present the benchmarking results for NusaX-senti, AfriHate, EthioEmo, and BRIGHTER, respectively. Results are reported for each language in the benchmark under training data settings of 100%, 50%, 10%, and 1%. To ensure stability and reliability, each experiment was run three times with different random seeds, and the reported results include the corresponding standard deviations. Based on the average results, our method yields better results than the other baselines, highlighting the performance of our method in cross-lingual settings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T8" class="ltx_table">
